  <h2 id="why-matters">Why this matters</h2>
  <p class="why-lede">Your gut isn't just digesting food. It's manufacturing the neurochemistry your brain runs on.</p>
  <ul class="why-list">
    <li><strong>About 90% of your body's serotonin</strong> — your mood, sleep, and appetite neurotransmitter — is produced in the gut</li>
    <li><strong>About 50% of your dopamine</strong> — motivation, reward, focus — is made there too</li>
    <li><strong>Roughly half of your norepinephrine</strong> — alertness and attention — originates in gut-adjacent organs</li>
    <li>Your gut microbiome also produces <strong>GABA</strong> (calm, sleep onset), <strong>acetylcholine</strong> (memory and learning), <strong>glutamate</strong>, and <strong>histamine</strong></li>
    <li><strong>The vagus nerve is the highway</strong> — about 90% of vagal signals go gut-to-brain, not brain-to-gut. Your gut is talking to your head far more than the other way around.</li>
    <li><strong>The damage cascade:</strong> leaky gut → systemic inflammation → blood-brain barrier permeability → neuroinflammation → "brain fog," mood drops, word-finding lapses</li>
    <li><strong>Why most probiotics fail:</strong> strain specificity, viability through stomach acid, CFU counts on labels vs. what actually colonizes — a "50 billion CFU" bottle that dies in the stomach is decoration, not medicine</li>
    <li>Gut-derived neuroinflammation is the most underestimated driver of early brain aging in men — and no hormone, peptide, or cognitive protocol reaches its ceiling on top of a broken gut</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="why-reveal">When we say <em>fix the gut</em>, we don't mean drink kombucha. We mean specific strains, specific delivery, specific protocol, run for a specific length of time — which is exactly what Biome NS Ultra (and Biome NS Rx for deeper cases) is built to do.</p>

  <MiniTwoPaths otcLabel="Order Biome NS Ultra" otcHref="/assessment" prompt="Ready to act?" />

  <h2>The Problem</h2>
  <p>
    When the gut lining becomes permeable, bacterial endotoxins enter the bloodstream,
    drive systemic inflammation, and degrade the blood-brain barrier. Most cognitive
    issues start in the gut — gut inflammation drives the gut-brain axis long before
    you feel it in your head.
  </p>
  <p>
    The microbiome is also central to visceral-fat regulation. A compromised biome
    degrades insulin sensitivity, short-chain fatty acid production, and bile-acid
    signaling — the exact metabolic levers that determine whether energy is stored as
    inflammatory visceral adipose tissue. That visceral fat then secretes its own
    inflammatory cytokines (IL-6, TNF-α) and amplifies circulating LPS endotoxin load,
    which further degrades tight junctions in the gut wall and the blood-brain barrier.
    The loop closes in the brain: neuroinflammation shows up as brain fog, word-finding
    lapses, and mood instability — which suppress the motivation needed to restore the
    microbiome in the first place. Microbiome, visceral fat, and cognition are not three
    problems. They are one loop.
  </p>

